%I A399801 #24 Sep 25 2026 17:01:33
%S A399801 1,2,3,5,6,8,10,11,12,14,15,17,18,20,21,23,24,26,28,29,30,32,34,35,36,
%T A399801 38,40,41,42,44,46,47,48,50,51,53,54,56,57,59,60,62,63,65,66,68,69,71,
%U A399801 72,74,75,77,78,80,81,83,84,86,87,89,90,92,93,95,96,98,100,101,102,104
%N A399801 a(1) = 1; a(n+1) is the smallest integer greater than a(n) such that: a(k) + a(k+2) is not a term for any k.
%C A399801 a(n) reaches the upper bound floor((3*n-1)/2) if and only if n = 1, n is even, or (n == 3 (mod 4) and floor(log_2(n)) is even).
%H A399801 Hoang Xuan Thanh, <a href="/A399801/a399801.pdf">Rigorous Proof of the Closed-Form Formula for the Greedy Sequence Avoiding a(k) + a(k+2)</a>
%F A399801 a(n) = floor((3*n-1)/2) - (n mod 2)*(m + (1-m)*(floor((n+1)/2) mod 2)) + [n=1], where m = floor(log_2(n)) mod 2.
%o A399801 (PARI) a(n) = my(m = logint(n,2)%2); floor((3*n-1)/2) - (n%2)*(m + (1-m)*(((n+1)\2)%2)) + (n==1)
%Y A399801 Cf. A016789 (even-indexed terms).
%Y A399801 Cf. Sequences avoiding a forbidden sum for any pair at distance k: A001651 (k=1) (upper bound), this sequence (k=2), A399866 (k=3), A399049 (k=4).
%Y A399801 Cf. Sequences avoiding a forbidden sum for any m consecutive terms: A001651 (m=2), A394576 (m=3), A394676 (m=4).
%K A399801 nonn,easy,new
%O A399801 1,2
%A A399801 _Hoang Xuan Thanh_, Sep 12 2026
